  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Increased decent work opportunities for young men and women in job-rich sectors and value chains in Sierra Leone</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In the past three decades, Sierra Leone&#x2019;s development has been affected by multiple crises, including the civil war from 1991 to 2002, the Ebola epidemic in 2014, and more recently the COVID-19 pandemic. In recent years, the economy has slowly started to recover, however, growth has not been inclusive. Poverty levels have increased from 53.8% in 2011 to 56.7% in 2018, while 10.8% of the population lives in abject poverty. Urban poverty is estimated at 41.2% while rural poverty is much higher than the national average at 72.4%. The COVID-19 pandemic and related mitigating effects have also disrupted service provision and economic activity in the country and are likely to have lasting impact on economic growth and development.Over 60% of Sierra Leone&#x2019;s population is composed of young women and men below the age of 35, and persons with disabilities represent 1.3% of the total population.  Sierra Leone&#x2019;s youth and persons with disabilities experience significant challenges related to lack of educational and employment opportunities &#x2013; particularly in rural areas. Furthermore, while over half of Sierra Leone&#x2019;s population is employed in agriculture, the sector is characterized by low levels of productivity, informality and a generalized absence of linkages with markets, inputs and support services. 
Limited access to these essential components is compounded by poorly maintained feeder road networks. Funding limitations, limited capacity to plan and supervise road rehabilitation work, poor technical knowledge and lack of road maintenance equipment are some of the persistent barriers that prevent road networks from being maintained.
The ILO&apos;s intervention which is part of a larger EU supported Jobs and Growth programme will stimulate job creation by improving the investment climate through enhanced access to finance, improving rural transport infrastructure, and upgrading human capital through general and vocational training and education.</narrative>
  </description>
